    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d206">

                <pb facs="0302" n="292" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg292"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/3–1945: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harriman</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-03-19T17:00:00+03:00">March 19, 1945—5
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received March 19—3:12 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>811. Sulzberger received his story from a source which he is pledged not to
                    reveal. From the way the Soviets (ReDeptel 566, March 12, <pb facs="0303"
                        n="293" xml:id="pg293"/> 3 p.m.) operate I am quite sure that the story did
                    not come from a Russian source and I have reason to believe Sulzberger based his
                    story on information received from a foreign Embassy here.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Harriman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
